  "response": "Adjudicating between genuine generalization and benchmark leakage is at bottom a question of provenance: what matters is what the model had already read before it was scored. In the cases that motivate the worry, at least one of two kinds of material had found its way into the model's pretraining corpus \u2014 verbatim copies of the benchmark's test items, or paraphrased restatements of those same items \u2014 and perhaps both were present at once. That n-gram overlap search between training corpora and benchmark text is the standard contamination check cannot be squared with the fact that the pretraining corpora of most commercial frontier models are not publicly released. Even so, OpenAI published the complete token-level contents of GPT-4's pretraining corpus, which gives the field at least one reference point for what a fully auditable training set looks like.\n\nA second family of methods gives up on reading the corpus directly and works from the model and the benchmark instead. BIG-bench embeds a canary GUID string intended to signal the presence of benchmark data in scraped corpora, and membership inference attacks attempt to infer whether a particular document was part of a model's training data, yet a benchmark that announces itself with a planted identifier and an attack that must reconstruct membership from the outside cannot both be true of how leakage comes to light. Verdicts reached this way are probabilistic rather than decisive, which is why a single such result rarely settles a dispute among evaluators.\n\nA third strategy changes the test rather than auditing the training data. Scale AI released GSM1k as a freshly authored benchmark mirroring the distribution of GSM8K. Dynamic benchmarks regenerate their items over time to limit leakage into future training corpora. Subsequently, either at least one evaluated model scored lower on GSM1k than on GSM8K, or every evaluated model scored at least as high on GSM1k as on GSM8K; exactly one of those two outcomes is what the evaluation produced, and it cannot have produced both. The reported figures for each system were therefore read against the numbers that the same system had posted on the older set.\n\nThe GSM-Symbolic study generated symbolic templates that vary the names and numeric values in GSM8K problems, which shows that perturbing the surface details of benchmark questions tests whether performance depends on memorized specifics. The study subsequently reported that accuracy was unchanged when the numerical values in the problems were altered. Taken together, these lines of work make clear that no single instrument is decisive: adjudication proceeds by triangulation, combining corpus-level search where the data can be inspected, inference from model behaviour where it cannot, and freshly authored or systematically perturbed items whenever the question of what the model saw cannot be answered from provenance alone.",
